last week's heatwave gripped britain as  record temperatures hit tinder dry  conditions fueled wildfires this was the  aftermath  buildings charred and homes destroyed  the uk's infrastructure just couldn't  cope as air and rail travel was  disrupted and in the days that followed  grass fires continued  this is just some of the devastation  caused by the extreme heat and now  according to new research that  blistering heat wave was made at least  10 times more likely because of climate  change although scientists say that's a  conservative estimate because extreme  temperatures are rising faster than  climate models predicted  and that's causing concern among experts  without climate change they calculated  that this would have been an event that  might have happened once in a thousand  years they've calculated that with  climate change it was an event that  would have only happened maybe one in a  hundred years but i think that they'll  find that actually it's far more common  even now due to climate change it's  showing that things are happening more  quickly than we expect  what kind of devastation could we see as  a result of all of this in the future  we know that for the next 30 years  things are only going to get worse  anyway  because it won't stop until the world  gets to net zero emissions  but it also means that we are in  uncharted territory and we're in for  some nasty shocks and last week might be  just the first of several nasty shocks  we are going to experience  while the uk saw a startling new reality  during the heat wave none of it should  come as a surprise as scientists now  warn the consequences of the climate  crisis on this type of weather could be  worse than previously thought aisha  zahid sky news
 

